; The facade's command line, end to end, as a shell session — the level
; SKILL.md §5 gives argument parsing, error messages and exit codes,
; and the level windtrap's own walkthrough teaches
; (examples/x-blueprint/test/cram). Every branch of [Windtrap.run] returns
; an exit code and the fixture applies it, so half the contract is an
; exit code, which is the one thing cram pins natively: a bare command
; asserts 0, and a trailing [N] asserts N.
;
; suite_main is the suite under the flags — five tests, no clock and no
; network, small enough for a session to pin whole streams. The deps
; line is load-bearing: without it dune would not rebuild the fixture
; before running a session, and every .t here would exercise stale code.

(executable
 (name suite_main)
 (modules suite_main)
 (libraries windtrap))

(cram
 (applies_to :whole_subtree)
 (deps suite_main.exe))

; Without dune. The instrumentation story holds by a test: nodune.t
; builds an instrumented library and a plain test executable with the
; toolchain's compiler alone — no dune and no ocamlfind in the session —
; against the installed windtrap, runs it from a scratch directory that
; is under no build directory, and reports with the installed binary.
; (package windtrap) is what installs the library and the binary and
; puts them where a findlib-less build finds them (OCAMLPATH, PATH); the
; two driver executables are the one-time step a findlib user makes
; once, a Ppxlib standalone linked against each backend.

(cram
 (applies_to nodune)
 (deps
  (package windtrap)
  coverage_ppx.exe
  mutate_ppx.exe))

(executable
 (name coverage_ppx)
 (modules coverage_ppx)
 (libraries ppxlib ppx_windtrap.coverage))

(executable
 (name mutate_ppx)
 (modules mutate_ppx)
 (libraries ppxlib ppx_windtrap.mutate))
